---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import JsonFormatter from '@/components/tools/dev/JsonFormatter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-formatter">
  <JsonFormatter client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to format JSON</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste your JSON into the input pane, or use <strong>Load file</strong> to open a <code>.json</code> file from disk.</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>Beautify</strong> and pick 2 spaces, 4 spaces or a tab. The output pane updates as you type.</li>
      <li>If the document is invalid, the error panel shows the line, column and character offset of the first problem, and the bad line with a caret under it.</li>
      <li>Switch to <strong>Minify</strong> to remove all optional bytes, or <strong>TypeScript</strong> to generate interfaces from the shape of the data.</li>
      <li>Copy the result, download it, or open the same document in the tree viewer for large payloads.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Reading the error message</h2>
    <p>
      JSON is a small grammar, so almost all parse failures have one of a few causes. The validator shows
      the raw engine message, plus a plain-English hint when it recognises the pattern.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>What you wrote</th><th>Why it fails</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": 1,&#125;</code></td><td>Trailing comma before <code>&#125;</code></td><td>Delete the comma</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;'a': 1&#125;</code></td><td>Single quotes are not JSON strings</td><td>Use double quotes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;a: 1&#125;</code></td><td>Unquoted key</td><td>Quote every key</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": 01&#125;</code></td><td>Leading zeros are not valid numbers</td><td>Write <code>1</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;"a": NaN&#125;</code></td><td><code>NaN</code> and <code>Infinity</code> are not JSON values</td><td>Use <code>null</code> or a string</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>// comment</code></td><td>JSON has no comments</td><td>Strip them, or use JSON5 in your own tooling</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Smart quotes <code>“a”</code></td><td>Pasted from a word processor</td><td>Retype with straight quotes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Beautify or minify?</h2>
    <p>
      Whitespace has no meaning in JSON, so both forms parse to the same value. Beautified JSON is for people:
      code review, debugging an API response, or a config file in version control where a line-based diff must
      be readable. Minified JSON is for machines: it removes all spaces and newlines, usually 15–30% of a
      formatted document before compression.
    </p>
    <p>
      Minifying changes the bytes your users download much less than you expect, because gzip and brotli
      already compress repeated indentation very well. Minify for storage limits and payload caps, not to make
      a compressed transport faster.
    </p>

    <h2>Sorting keys for comparison</h2>
    <p>
      Object member order has no meaning in JSON, but a text diff does not know that. Two API responses with
      identical content can show many false changes because the server wrote the keys in a different order.
      Turn on <strong>Sort keys A→Z</strong> on both documents before you diff them. Array order has meaning,
      and the tool never changes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Generating TypeScript interfaces</h2>
    <p>
      TypeScript mode reads the parsed value and writes one exported interface per distinct nested object,
      named after the key that contains it. For arrays it merges the shapes of all elements: a field in all
      elements is required, a field in only some is optional with <code>?</code>, and a field with more than
      one type becomes a union. Empty arrays give <code>unknown[]</code>, because an empty sample says nothing
      about the element type.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use the output as a first draft. Generated types describe the sample you pasted, not the contract of the
      API. A field that is non-null in your sample can be nullable in production. Compare the interfaces with
      the API documentation before you commit them.
    </p>

    <h2>Escaping and unescaping</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Escape</strong> turns the whole input into one JSON string literal, with quotes. Use it to put a
      JSON document inside another JSON document, as webhook payloads and log fields often require.
      <strong>Unescape</strong> reverses it and turns <code>"&#123;\"a\":1&#125;"</code> back into a document
      you can format.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The JSON formatter runs as JavaScript in your browser tab and sends nothing, so you can paste API
      responses with customer records, internal identifiers or bearer tokens. After the first load, it also
      works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
